For \( Q(x) = (x-a)(x-b) \), the probability that both \( a, b \in S \) is: \[
\frac{\binom{n}{4}}{\binom{n+1}{6}} = \frac{15}{(n+1)(n)}
\]

Summing over all pairs \( (a, b) \) with \( a, b \neq 0 \) and \( a \neq b \), the probability is: \[
\sum_{1 \leq a < b \leq n} \frac{15}{n(n+1)} = \frac{15 \binom{n}{2}}{n(n+1)} = \frac{15n(n-1)}{2n(n+1)} = \frac{15(n-1)}{2(n+1)}
\]

For \( Q(x) = (x-a)(x-b)(x-c) \), the probability that \( a, b, c \in S \) is: \[
\frac{\binom{n}{3}}{\binom{n+1}{6}} = \frac{20}{(n+1)n(n-1)}
\]

Summing over all triples \( (a, b, c) \) with \( a, b, c \neq 0 \) and all distinct, the probability is: \[
\sum_{1 \leq a < b < c \leq n} \frac{20}{n(n+1)(n-1)} = \frac{20 \binom{n}{3}}{n(n+1)(n-1)} = \frac{20n(n-1)(n-2)/6}{n(n+1)(n-1)} = \frac{10(n-2)}{3(n+1)}
\]

